  "step_2": "The individual considered the two lotteries and noted that the chance of winning was nearly equal in both (implying similar probabilities of the favorable outcome). They emphasized that the potential win in Lottery A was higher than in Lottery B. The justification for the choice centers on the higher potential gain in Lottery A, despite the similar probability of winning. There is no mention of the distribution of probabilities, skewness, or any evaluation of whether more probability mass is assigned to favorable outcomes. The individual focused solely on the magnitude of the favorable outcome and the near-equality of the winning probabilities.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery has more probability mass assigned to favorable outcomes (i.e., is more left-skewed). The individual did not consider the relative probability mass on favorable outcomes; instead, they compared the size of the favorable outcome and noted the near-equality of probabilities. The individual’s justification was based on the higher potential gain in Lottery A, not on the skewness or the distribution of probabilities. The information considered by the individual (favorable outcome size and approximate probability equality) does not align with the information required by the reason. Furthermore, the reasoning process described (favoring higher payoff despite similar probabilities) contradicts the reason’s preference for the more left-skewed lottery, which would be Lottery B due to its slightly higher probability of winning. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason to make the decision.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
